🚀 Main Command (7-day retention)
velero schedule create daily-backup \
  --schedule="0 2 * * *" \
  --ttl 168h \
  --include-namespaces default
🧠 Simple Explanation (line by line)
🔹 velero schedule create daily-backup

👉 Create a scheduled backup job
👉 daily-backup = name of the schedule

🔹 --schedule="0 2 * * *"

👉 This is cron format (time scheduling)

0 2 * * *
│ │ │ │ │
│ │ │ │ └── Every day of week
│ │ │ └──── Every month
│ │ └────── Every day
│ └──────── 2 AM
└────────── 0 minutes

👉 Meaning:

Run backup every day at 2:00 AM

🔹 --ttl 168h

👉 TTL = Time To Live

168 hours = 7 days

👉 After 7 days:

Backup automatically deleted ✅

🔹 --include-namespaces default

👉 Backup only default namespace

👉 You can change:

default → specific app
production → prod workloads
remove it → backup entire cluster
🎯 What this command does (full picture)
Every day at 2 AM:
   ↓
Take backup of default namespace
   ↓
Store in cloud (Azure / AWS)
   ↓
Keep only 7 days
   ↓
Auto delete old backups
🔍 Verify everything
Check schedule
velero schedule get
Check backups
velero backup get
Describe schedule
velero schedule describe daily-backup
🏭 Production version (better)
velero schedule create prod-daily-backup \
  --schedule="0 1 * * *" \
  --ttl 168h \
  --include-namespaces production

👉 Runs at 1 AM for production apps

💪 Interview-ready explanation

You can say:

“We configured Velero scheduled backups using cron expressions. The backups run daily at 2 AM with a TTL of 168 hours, ensuring only the last 7 days of backups are retained automatically. This reduces storage cost and ensures disaster recovery readiness.”

🚀 Bonus (optional but powerful)
Backup entire cluster
velero schedule create full-backup \
  --schedule="0 3 * * *" \
  --ttl 168h
Backup multiple namespaces
velero schedule create multi-backup \
  --schedule="0 2 * * *" \
  --ttl 168h \
  --include-namespaces default,production
